{"kind":"request","text":"Design note: retained files. Write notes/\u003cdate\u003e-retained-files.md.\n\nBackground. Hugh's renovation example, 2026-10-21: he and his agent made clean drawings of a space from tape measurements and phone photos, then sent the PDFs to a contractor by email and asked for his availability. The whole output of the drawing session would have been fine to share, but not by email; the contractor will connect his own agent, and that agent wants the base geometry, not the PDF. The process starts with the customer and with file sharing, in the customer's files or in a private chat session. Hugh asked how that generalizes. The planner's answer: a matter room any person creates from their own chat, in which \"share this with Dan\" is a recorded act and email is only the channel; the provider joins later and installs his package into the same room. The first missing piece is capture: bytes by digest with a stated source, readable only by the room's members.\n\nWhat exists (main a9094eb1). Reads of a scope go through one Readers port. In a deployment that is a read session, issued by the membership scope to an active member's device key, bound to one repository, for at most 600 seconds; or a signed read. Retained inputs are served by kind and digest under that check with one more rule: the reader must hold an authorized entry that names that exact digest (packages/scope/src/reads.ts, #names and #carried). The readable kinds are definition, entry, rule, text, snapshot and value. A detached text is at most 64 KiB, a retained read at most 1 MiB, and retained inputs live in the scope's SQLite tables retained_input and retained_value. There is no binary kind. The only object store in the room Worker is APPLICATION_CONTENT, which holds signed application packets; its route serves screen assets by directory and install address with no session check. The repository-import note (d7b9e504, draft 1) proposes an R2 reference shape for copied attachments: byte domain, digest, size, object identity, access policy, retention owner, \"a URL alone is insufficient\", extending the retained-input resolver. Nothing of it is landed. Custody narrower than a room is not designed anywhere and is out of this note's scope.\n\nDecision (Hugh, 2026-10-21, about 08:45 ET). Add one retained-input kind for files, stored in R2 on Cloudflare, or the equivalent object store when self-hosted. He asked that the note clearly distinguish the cases where object storage is the right choice from the cases where an Artifacts or Git repository is.\n\nThe distinction the note must settle. dap already has two homes for bytes, and the file kind extends one of them; it does not add a third category.\n\n- The journal says what happened: entries and the small inputs they retain.\n- The repository (Artifacts or GitHub) holds what the room publishes: its work product, as versions of one tree, written only by the destination with a receipt, public by address, cloned by members, reviewed by exact version.\n- Retained inputs hold what entries name: bytes an act carried or depended on, immutable, private to the room's readers, with no tree and no history. A new version is a new digest named by a new entry.\n\nA file is retained when an entry names it as an input or as evidence, when it must stay private to the room's readers, when it never changes in place, or when it is larger than the repository's limits (32 MB a blob and 1 GB a repository on Artifacts). A file is published when it is a version of the room's work product, when it needs a public address or a clone, or when a tree of paths is the thing being reviewed. A file can be both: retained first by a capture, and published later by an act that names the retained digest; that extension of publish-file is a follow-up, not part of this note. The reasons not to put evidence in the repository: published files are public to anyone holding the directory ID; every write is a destination commit with a reservation and a receipt; the file is attributed to the destination's commit, not to the person who captured it; the archive and the portable clone treat text and private custody as export-policy decisions, never as readable refs. The reasons not to put work product in object storage: no paths in relation to each other, no diff, no versions, no public address, no clone. Where a retained file's bytes live, SQLite below a threshold or an object store above it, is a keeper choice, not a semantic one. The note should state the rule as \"retained or published\", never \"small or large\".\n\nProposed shape, for the note to confirm or change.\n\n1. Field type. { type: \"file\"; max: number; media?: readonly string[] }, always detached. The intent carries the digest, the size and the media type; the scope keeps the bytes as a retained input of the kind file in a new byte domain named like artroom-text-1. Guards, rules, index sends and sends to a scope that is not a lane may not read it, by the same redactable-read rule as text. The slot keeps the digest. A redact effect removes the bytes and writes the tombstone, as for text.\n\n2. Keeper port. put if absent by domain and digest with size and media type; get as a stream; head; delete for redaction and for the orphan sweep. Implementations: the R2 binding on Cloudflare; S3-compatible and Google Cloud Storage when self-hosted, which celld's fleet bucket already covers; and the existing SQLite tables below a size threshold, so a small deployment needs no bucket. One keeper per deployment in the first version, no keeper per room. No public route to the bucket. The entry names domain and digest only, never the keeper, so a journal moved to another deployment needs its files moved with it; the note states this.\n\n3. Write path. Two forms. Beside the intent, like texts, up to a bound the builder measures against the Worker's request-body limit. Or a session-authenticated upload route, PUT /v1/scopes/:scope/retained/file/:digest, that stores the bytes before the act is signed and answers once the digest and size are verified; the act then names the digest, and at judgment the scope checks that the keeper holds that digest with the declared size and media type, else refuses bad-field as a mismatched text is refused. Unreferenced uploads are swept after a window. The order is the import note's: write the immutable object, verify digest and size, then commit the reference in the entry; a failed commit leaves an orphan the sweep removes; there is no cross-service transaction.\n\n4. Read path. GET /v1/scopes/:scope/retained/file/:digest under the existing Readers port and the \"an authorized entry names this digest\" rule, streamed, with the declared media type, cache-control private, x-content-type-options nosniff, and a policy that prevents rendering as a page. Signed reads apply as for other retained kinds. Never served through /site/. Range requests are an open question.\n\n5. Budgets. A capacity dimension of its own for retained file bytes a scope holds, charged at the same sites as the five existing dimensions; a per-file maximum; a per-deployment keeper quota. The journal's scopeBytes is not charged for file bytes.\n\n6. Replay. A file is a retained input: missing gives incomplete, redacted names the tombstone, integrity mode needs no bytes. Each HistorySource needs a file source or a stated absence.\n\n7. Archive and export. The journal archive holds the digest only, extending section 15.1 point 4 of the durability note from text to files. The portable clone's export policy covers files as it covers text. No file export in this note.\n\n8. Provenance. The platform piece stops at bytes, digest, size and media type. Source, derivation and the original file name are the application's fields: a capture act in the matter package names a source text, the fact references of the inputs it was derived from, and a file name. The note gives the recommended field shape so that packages agree, and does not design the matter package.\n\nOpen questions the note answers or names: the beside-the-intent bound and the upload threshold, measured; whether the SQLite tier is in the first slice; Range reads; what happens to files when a scope reaches its final state or a new incarnation; a keeper per room, for a customer's own bucket, named and deferred.\n\nFirst users. The matter room's capture act (use cases 29 and 30); the bid response's import, now a link and a digest (49); the learning project's recordings (47); the repository import's attachment copy (d7b9e504).\n\nAcceptance. The note states the retained-or-published rule with its test and the reasons; the field type, the byte domain, the keeper port and its three implementations, the write and read paths, budgets, replay, archive and redaction; names each bound to measure; names the follow-ups (publish from a retained file, custody narrower than a room, a keeper per room, the matter package); and orders the builder's slices with the capture act of a matter room as the exit test.","body":{"conditions":"Document only; checker review before landing; no comparisons; product names only where a keeper or host implementation is listed, otherwise none but GitHub; implementation requests follow after the note lands.","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}